--   This block constructs a concrete metric space, analytic and operator K-groups, and a homomorphism called its constructed coarse assembly map. For j≥0, the triangular lattice disc consists of u=(a,b)∈ℤ² with ρ(u)=max(|a|,|b|,|a+b|)≤j; adjacent distinct vertices differ by at most one in each of a, b, and a+b. Above u lies SL₃(ℤ/2^{j−ρ(u)}ℤ). Reduction maps connect these fibers, and holonomy at level k is diag(t,t⁻¹,1) modulo 2ᵏ, for a chosen transcendental 2-adic unit t congruent to 1 modulo 2. Fiber edges use a symmetric finite set of SL₃(ℤ) moves within one base vertex, or the identity move across adjacent base vertices; at every common quotient level their coordinates satisfy y=sx·holonomy(t)^charge. The charge is +1 when crossing from b<0 to b=0 at positive first coordinate, −1 for the reverse crossing, and zero otherwise. The final move set consists of elementary transvections with coefficient ±1 and the identity. Each finite component uses shortest-path distance when connected and the discrete 0–1 metric otherwise. Components are joined metrically by d(x,oᵢ)+i+j+2+d(oⱼ,y), with oⱼ the identity fiber point over (j,0). Uniform finite balls means that, for each nonnegative radius, all closed balls have a common finite cardinality bound. On ℓ²(X,H), operator entries are coordinate compressions; propagation at most r means entries vanish when d(x,y)>r. The Roe algebra is the norm-closed nonunital star algebra generated by finite-propagation operators whose entries and adjoint entries are compact. With finite balls these generators already form an algebra. The block supplies uniformly bounded block operators, flattening of iterated ℓ² sums, zero-extension isometries, conjugation T↦VTV*, and extension F↦1+V(F−1)V*, including Roe homomorphisms for injective distance-nonincreasing maps and embeddings of separable Hilbert spaces into ℓ²(ℕ,ℂ). An odd analytic cycle over a nonunital C*-algebra A comprises a separable complex Hilbert space, a possibly nonunital representation π, and a bounded operator F for which [F,π(a)], (F²−1)π(a), and (F*−F)π(a) are compact for every a∈A. OddK(A) is the free abelian group on these cycles modulo degenerate cycles, unitary equivalence, direct-sum additivity, and norm-continuous homotopies. Locally finite K₁(X) is OddK(C₀(X,ℂ)); continuous maps preserving escape from compact sets act by precomposition of representations. Rips points at scale r are finitely supported probability weights whose support has diameter at most r, with coordinatewise topology; these spaces are compact for finite X, and increasing integer scales define KX₁(X) as the direct limit of their locally finite groups. Operator K₁(A) is the kernel of the scalar map from the topological K₁ of the unitization to K₁(ℂ), where topological K₁ is presented by invertible matrices modulo multiplication, stabilization, and continuous paths. Roe multipliers preserve the Roe algebra under multiplication on either side. For a self-adjoint Roe multiplier F with F²−1 in the Roe algebra, the unitary −((F−i)(F+i)⁻¹)² differs from 1 by a Roe element and defines a relative K₁ class. Compact perturbation, symmetric compression, completion by a character, and a Cayley-based bounded transform produce unital self-adjoint cycle families with operator norms at most one. Represented square roots of Rips coordinates localize these cycles; embedding their coefficients into ℓ²(ℕ,ℂ) yields operators with propagation at most 2r, uniformly bounded norms, and compact square defects, whose block sum supplies the boundary class. At scale r, functions on component i are extended by zero when i>r and mapped to zero otherwise. The resulting free-cycle index descends to OddK only if it annihilates the defining relations; its definition returns zero otherwise. Likewise, the sequence of scale indices is used only if compatible with coarsening, and is replaced by the zero family otherwise. Lifting this compatible family to the direct limit and reindexing the concrete vertex union defines constructedCoarseAssembly: KX₁(VertexUnion)→K₁(TotalRoe), with TotalRoe using coefficient space ℓ²(ℕ,ℂ).
